<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · city</p><h1 class="enc-title">Bandar Abbas</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Bandar Abbas is a port on the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/persian-gulf">Persian Gulf</a> coast of southern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/iran">Iran</a>, named after <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/abbas-i-of-persia">Shah Abbas I</a>, who developed it after capturing <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hormuz">Hormuz</a> from the Portuguese in 1622. It became Iran's main port for trade with European companies. It was important for the trade and naval ambitions of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/afsharid-empire">Afsharid Empire</a> under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nader-shah">Nader Shah</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/bandar-abbas.jpg" alt="Bandar Abbas" width="330" height="220" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Abed · GFDL · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Bandarabbas-sahel-2.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Setting</h2><p>Bandar Abbas stands on flat ground about 9 metres above sea level on the southern coast of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/iran">Iran</a>, at the narrow Strait of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hormuz">Hormuz</a>, just across from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/oman">Oman</a>'s Musandam. The island of Qeshm lies to the south, and the nearest hills are Mount Geno, 17 kilometres to the north. Because it has always been a port, its names have reflected that function. The commonest was Gamrūn, which Henry Yule derives either from gümrük, &quot;customhouse&quot;, or, which he found more convincing, from Persian kamrūn, &quot;shrimp&quot;. The present name means &quot;Port of Abbas&quot;, after Abbas the Great, who reigned from 1588 to 1629.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>The earliest record of the place is from the reign of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/darius-i">Darius the Great</a>, between 522 and 486 BCE, when his commander Scylax embarked from here for <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/india">India</a> and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/red-sea">Red Sea</a>. The Portuguese conquered it in 1514 and made it an important base for protecting their commerce in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/persian-gulf">Persian Gulf</a> and India, calling it Comorão for the lobsters and crabs on its shores. Shah Abbas took it from them in 1614, and in 1622 he defeated Portuguese troops in the region with the help of the English Navy and the Persian commander Imam Quli Khan. The city was renamed Bandar-e Abbas, and with English naval backing he developed it into a major port.</p><p>In 1625 a combined Anglo-<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dutch">Dutch</a> fleet attacked the Portuguese and took the trade posts. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dutch-east-india-company">Dutch East India Company</a> soon outcompeted the English, and from 1654 it controlled the local spice and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/silk">silk</a> trade completely until 1765. From 1794 to 1868 the city was held by the Sultanate of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/oman">Oman</a> and Zanzibar under a lease from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/persia">Persia</a>, whose Arabic and Persian versions apparently differed. Persia tried to oust the Omanis in 1823 and recovered the city in 1854. Under British pressure after the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/anglo-persian-war">Anglo-Persian War</a> of 1856 the lease was renewed, and in 1868 it was renewed once more and cancelled two months later by the Persian government.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">The place today</h2><p>An earthquake in July 1902 damaged parts of the city. Under Mohammad Reza Shah <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/middle-persian">Pahlavi</a> the government invested heavily in the port: in 1955 it was a small fishing port of about 17,000 people, and in 1977 the bulk of the Iranian Navy was moved from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/khorramshahr">Khorramshahr</a> to a new headquarters and naval base here. The city is the capital of Hormozgan Province and has been the southern terminus of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/iran">Iran</a>'s main north-south railway since 1993. Its original inhabitants speak Bandari, a variety of Persian. Its pottery was once exported to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/england">England</a> as goombroon ware.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Bandar Abbas” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>The Persian Gulf trade through Bushehr and <b>Bandar Abbas</b> continued, and Nader's intervention in Oman, which ended in the capture of Muscat around 1743, extended Iranian reach into the Arabian Sea.</blockquote><a href="/empire/afsharid-empire">Afsharid Empire · Socioeconomy</a></li><li><blockquote>In 1622 an Anglo-Persian force under Shah Abbas I of the Safavid Empire captured the island, ending the kingdom; trade moved to <b>Bandar Abbas</b>.</blockquote><a href="/state/kingdom-of-ormus">Kingdom of Ormus</a></li><li><blockquote>Gwadar on the Makran coast was acquired in the 1780s and <b>Bandar Abbas</b> leased from Persia.</blockquote><a href="/state/omani-empire">Omani Empire</a></li></ul></section>
